</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section reviews literature in three key areas: hypergraph generation methods, the emerging use of LLMs for simulating human behavior, and efforts to apply LLMs to network generation.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Hypergraph Generation Methods</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Research in hypergraph generation aims to create synthetic networks that faithfully reproduce the structural and temporal patterns of real-world systems. Existing methods can be broadly categorized into static and dynamic approaches. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee and Kang, 2025</a>)</cite>
<span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Static generators</span> construct a hypergraph in its entirety based on prescribed global or local properties. These include extensions of classical graph models, such as configuration models that preserve fine-grained local statistics like joint degree distributions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nakajima et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, and block models designed to embed community structures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
<span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Dynamic generators</span> simulate the evolutionary process of a hypergraph, often by adding nodes and hyperedges incrementally. A prominent example is the extension of preferential attachment to hypergraphs, where new nodes are more likely to connect to well-connected existing nodes, thereby generating heavy-tailed degree distributions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Do et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. Other dynamic models are inspired by real-world analogies, such as a forest fire, to determine hyperedge formation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kook et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>.
A separate line of work focuses on generating representative <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">sub-hypergraphs</span> from massive networks via biased sampling techniques <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choe et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
While these models have advanced our ability to replicate structural patterns, they often rely on hand-engineered rules and statistical assumptions, limiting their capacity to capture the underlying semantic drivers of hypergraph formation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>LLMs for Human Behavior Simulation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The emergence of Large Language Models (LLMs) has opened new frontiers in simulating complex human behaviors. Prior work has demonstrated LLMs’ abilities to realistically simulate human responses and interactions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aher et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Argyle et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, including nuanced aspects like agent decision-making <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, cognitive biases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ashery et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and emotional responses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. More recently, research has studied the dynamics of LLM agent populations, showing how social conventions and consensus can arise from their interactions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ashery et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marzo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. However, while these studies often simulate interactions over social networks, they tend to make simplistic assumptions about the underlying network structure or require human involvement to build it <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chuang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. This body of work suggests that if LLMs can successfully model the individual and collective behaviors, they should also be capable of generating the resulting network structures themselves.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>LLMs for Complex Network Generation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The application of LLMs to graph-structured data is a nascent but rapidly developing field <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Much of the current research focuses on leveraging LLMs for graph-related tasks, such as reasoning over graph structures in natural language <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, performing node classification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and enhancing knowledge graph applications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. A few contemporaneous works have explored using LLMs for social network generation, but have concentrated on traditional pairwise graphs, analyzing the emergence of properties like scale-free degree distributions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marzo et al., 2023</a>)</cite> or fundamental principles like preferential attachment and homophily <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Papachristou and Yuan, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Other studies have investigated the risks of demographic and social bias in LLM-based graph generation, revealing that models tend to significantly overestimate political homophily <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. While some research has begun to investigate whether LLMs can comprehend hypergraphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, the direct generation of complex, realistic hypergraphs using LLMs remains a significant and largely unexplored challenge. Our work seeks to bridge this gap.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section provides the formal definitions and mathematical notation for hypergraphs used throughout this paper.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A hypergraph is formally defined as a pair <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\{v_{1},v_{2},\ldots,v_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\{v_{1},v_{2},\ldots,v_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite set of vertices, and <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=\{e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=\{e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of hyperedges. Each hyperedge <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}\subseteq V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}\subseteq V</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-empty subset of vertices, allowing it to connect any number of nodes. The number of vertices <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=|V|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=|V|</annotation></semantics></math> is the <span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">order</span> of the hypergraph, and the number of hyperedges <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=|E|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=|E|</annotation></semantics></math> is its <span id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">size</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">cardinality</span> of a hyperedge <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|e_{i}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|e_{i}|</annotation></semantics></math>, is the number of vertices it contains. A hypergraph is said to be <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-uniform if all its hyperedges have the same cardinality <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. The <span id="S3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">degree</span> of a vertex <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of hyperedges that contain it, defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(v)=\sum_{e\in E}\mathbb{I}(v\in e)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(v)=\sum_{e\in E}\mathbb{I}(v\in e)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator function.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The structure of a hypergraph can be represented by an <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times m</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">incidence matrix</span> <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, where its entries are defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}_{ij}=\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{if }v_{i}\in e_{j}\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}_{ij}=\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{if }v_{i}\in e_{j}\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">This matrix provides a complete, unambiguous representation of the relationships between vertices and hyperedges. Higher-order relationships can also be represented by an adjacency tensor, which generalizes the concept of an adjacency matrix to capture multi-way interactions, although the incidence matrix is sufficient for the scope of this work.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.11728v1/Fig.1.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/257;" width="476" height="257" alt="The schematic diagram of the research motivation of this article.">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>. </span><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The schematic diagram of the research motivation of this article.</span><span id="acmlabel1" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">The schematic diagram of the research motivation of this article.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Observations and Analysis</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent studies have shown that Large Language Models (LLMs) can effectively simulate human behavior, emotions, and social computation. Inspired by this, we investigate whether LLMs can also generate high-quality, realistic hypergraphs. In this section, we identify eight structural and dynamic patterns consistently observed in real-world hypergraphs. We then introduce a mathematical model, inspired by mechanisms in pairwise networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pagan et al., 2021</a>)</cite> but extended to high-order interactions, to explain the "rich get richer" phenomenon. This model not only provides a theoretical foundation for the observed heavy-tailed distributions but also offers a degree of interpretability for the success of LLMs in simulating hypergraph generation.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Universal Patterns in Real-World Hypergraphs</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our analysis of diverse real-world hypergraphs reveals eight universal patterns. Several of these, such as the distributions of degrees, hyperedge sizes, and intersection sizes, are illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Modeling Hypergraph Using Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. These patterns serve as structural and dynamic benchmarks for synthetic hypergraph generation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P1. Heavy-tailed degree distribution:</span>  The number of hyperedges connected to a node follows a heavy-tailed distribution, indicating the existence of "hub" nodes that participate in many interactions, a phenomenon commonly described as "the rich get richer."</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P2. Heavy-tailed hyperedge size distribution:</span>  The number of nodes within hyperedges is also heavy-tailed. While most interactions occur in small groups, a few massive collaborations or events encompass a large number of nodes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P3. Heavy-tailed intersection size distribution:</span>  The number of shared nodes between pairs of hyperedges is heavy-tailed. Most hyperedge pairs have no or very small overlaps, but a few have large intersections, indicating significant structural correlations.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kook et al., 2020</a>)</cite></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P4. Skewed singular value distribution:</span>  The singular values of the hypergraph’s incidence matrix exhibit a skewed, heavy-tailed distribution. This implies that the hypergraph’s structure is low-rank, with a few dominant components capturing most of the structural information. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Drineas et al., 2004</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sarkar and Dong, 2011</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2012</a>)</cite></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P5. Heavy-tailed group degree distribution:</span>  The distribution of community or group sizes is heavy-tailed. This reflects a hierarchical community structure with a few large, overarching communities and many small, tight-knit groups. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Benson et al., 2018</a>)</cite></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P6. Temporal locality of hypergraph evolution:</span>  The formation of new hyperedges is temporally localized. Nodes that have participated in recent interactions are more likely to participate in future ones, demonstrating short-term memory in the evolutionary process. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Benson et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gallo et al., 2024</a>)</cite></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P7. Power-law persistence:</span>  The inter-event times of a node’s participations in hyperedges follow a power-law distribution. This pattern signifies bursty dynamics, with long inactive periods punctuated by intense flurries of activity. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choo and Shin, 2022</a>)</cite></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P8. Diminishing overlaps:</span>  The density of interactions tends to decrease over time. As a hypergraph evolves, newly formed hyperedges are less likely to overlap with existing ones. The density of interactions is defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="DoI(G_{t}):=\frac{|\{\{e_{i},e_{j}\}\mid e_{i},e_{j}\in E_{t},e_{i}\cap e_{j}\neq\emptyset\}|}{|\{\{e_{i},e_{j}\}\mid e_{i},e_{j}\in E_{t}\}|}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>e</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>e</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mi>e</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>e</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>e</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">DoI(G_{t}):=\frac{|\{\{e_{i},e_{j}\}\mid e_{i},e_{j}\in E_{t},e_{i}\cap e_{j}\neq\emptyset\}|}{|\{\{e_{i},e_{j}\}\mid e_{i},e_{j}\in E_{t}\}|}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p9.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of hyperedges at time <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. The numerator is the number of intersecting pairs of hyperedges, and the denominator is the total number of pairs. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kook et al., 2020</a>)</cite></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">These eight patterns are intuitively or directly connected to our generative mechanism. The proposed mathematical model, a more stringent form of the power-law, provides a unified explanation for these heavy-tailed phenomena.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>A Microscopic Model for High-Order Preferential Attachment</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To explain the prevalent heavy-tailed distributions, we develop a generative model based on microscopic dynamics. This model extends preferential attachment from pairwise graphs to high-order hypergraphs, incorporating node quality and collaborative inertia.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a hypergraph <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=|V|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=|V|</annotation></semantics></math> nodes. Each node <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> possesses an intrinsic quality, and we rank them accordingly, with <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="rank_{i}\in\{1,...,N\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">rank_{i}\in\{1,...,N\}</annotation></semantics></math> representing the rank of node <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> (where 1 is the best). The model also includes a <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">collaborative inertia or activation threshold</span> parameter <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the model simplifies to the classic Zipf law.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">At each time step, a new hyperedge of size <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is generated. An "initiator" node is chosen. The remaining <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math> collaborators are then selected based on a preferential attachment mechanism. The probability of selecting a node <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> depends on its rank <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the inertia <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. We define an effective reachability probability for collaborator selection:</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{reach}}(i)=\frac{(r_{i}+\alpha)^{-\gamma}}{\sum_{j=1}^{N}(r_{j}+\alpha)^{-\gamma}},\quad\gamma&gt;0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>reach</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{reach}}(i)=\frac{(r_{i}+\alpha)^{-\gamma}}{\sum_{j=1}^{N}(r_{j}+\alpha)^{-\gamma}},\quad\gamma&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, we introduce a <span id="S4.SS2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">quality filter</span>, asserting that a hyperedge is only successfully formed if all selected nodes <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> have a quality higher than a certain threshold <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{th}</annotation></semantics></math>. This reflects the fact that high-order collaboration requires a minimum quality commitment from all participants.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> be the degree of node <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. The probability that node <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is chosen as a collaborator in a single step is:</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P_{\text{sel}}(i)=\mathbb{I}[q_{i}&gt;q_{th}]\cdot\frac{(r_{i}+\alpha)^{-\gamma}}{\sum_{j:q_{j}&gt;q_{th}}(r_{j}+\alpha)^{-\gamma}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>sel</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{sel}}(i)=\mathbb{I}[q_{i}&gt;q_{th}]\cdot\frac{(r_{i}+\alpha)^{-\gamma}}{\sum_{j:q_{j}&gt;q_{th}}(r_{j}+\alpha)^{-\gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}[\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}[\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator function for the quality filter. If we assume a constant rate of hyperedge arrival <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, the degree evolution can be described by the continuous-time master equation:</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d}{dt}\mathbb{E}[d_{i}(t)]=\lambda\cdot P_{\text{sel}}(i)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>sel</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{dt}\mathbb{E}[d_{i}(t)]=\lambda\cdot P_{\text{sel}}(i)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Assuming the system reaches a steady state after a long time <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, the expected degree of node <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is:</p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}=\mathbb{E}[d_{i}(T)]=\lambda T\cdot P_{\text{sel}}(i)=\lambda T\cdot\frac{(r_{i}+\alpha)^{-\gamma}}{\sum_{j\in I^{*}}(r_{j}+\alpha)^{-\gamma}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>sel</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}=\mathbb{E}[d_{i}(T)]=\lambda T\cdot P_{\text{sel}}(i)=\lambda T\cdot\frac{(r_{i}+\alpha)^{-\gamma}}{\sum_{j\in I^{*}}(r_{j}+\alpha)^{-\gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of high-quality nodes that pass the filter. This simplifies to the Zipf-Mandelbrot form:</p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}=A\cdot(r_{i}+\alpha)^{-\gamma}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}=A\cdot(r_{i}+\alpha)^{-\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\frac{\lambda T}{\sum_{j\in I^{*}}(r_{j}+\alpha)^{-\gamma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\frac{\lambda T}{\sum_{j\in I^{*}}(r_{j}+\alpha)^{-\gamma}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a normalization constant. For <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the denominator can be approximated by a Hurwitz zeta function or, for large <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, by <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ln((N+\alpha)/\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ln((N+\alpha)/\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This derivation shows how the Zipf-Mandelbrot law can emerge from microscopic dynamics. This model is not merely abstract; its components have conceptual parallels in our HyperLLM framework. For instance, a node’s rank (<math id="S4.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) relates to its perceived influence, the inertia (<math id="S4.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>) is reflected in review thresholds, and the quality filter (<math id="S4.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) is enacted by agents like the <span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reviewer</span> and <span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remover</span>. This correspondence suggests our agent-based system succeeds by implicitly simulating such fundamental mechanisms to ensure structural fidelity.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.11728v1/Fig.2.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/227;" width="476" height="227" alt="The core mechanism of multi-agent collaborative hypergraph generation.">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>. </span><span id="S4.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The core mechanism of multi-agent collaborative hypergraph generation.</span><span id="acmlabel2" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">The core mechanism of multi-agent collaborative hypergraph generation.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Hypergraph Generators</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our proposed framework, HyperLLM, generates hypergraphs through a process that reflects real-world network development. It consists of two key stages: a Construction Phase and an Evolution Phase. The construction phase uses an efficient iterative algorithm to quickly build the initial network. Then, the evolution phase applies a multi-agent system (MAS), where four specialized LLM-powered agents collaboratively refine and expand the hypergraph, maintaining both structural coherence and semantic depth.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>High-order Language Prompts</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At the core of HyperLLM are carefully engineered prompts that harness the reasoning capabilities of LLMs. Our framework operates on nodes conceptualized as rich entities, each possessing a unique set of attributes and a persona. The prompts for the four agents in the evolution phase are designed to be generic, allowing HyperLLM to be adapted across various domains, from social and biological networks to scientific collaborations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) Generator Agent Template:</span> This agent is tasked with creating new hyperedges by identifying potential collaborations centered around a specific entity. The prompt guides the LLM to consider both entity attributes and the existing network topology.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.SS1.p3.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="259.3" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 259.3" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,259.3) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 253.4 C 0 256.66 2.64 259.3 5.91 259.3 L 471.47 259.3 C 474.73 259.3 477.38 256.66 477.38 253.4 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 253.4 C 1.97 255.57 3.73 257.33 5.91 257.33 L 471.47 257.33 C 473.65 257.33 475.41 255.57 475.41 253.4 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 11.44 8.67)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:32.85em;--ltx-fo-height:17.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="241.96" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 241.96)" width="454.55"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS1.p3.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:32.85em;">
<span id="S5.SS1.p3.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">System prompt:<span id="S5.SS1.p3.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> You are a hypergraph relationship generator. Your task is to form a new collaborative group around a central entity.</span></span></span>
<span id="S5.SS1.p3.pic1.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.pic1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">User content:<span id="S5.SS1.p3.pic1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
A new collaboration is being formed in a [Domain] network.</span></span></span>
<span id="S5.SS1.p3.pic1.1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.pic1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Central Entity:<span id="S5.SS1.p3.pic1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> [Entity ID]</span></span></span>
<span id="S5.SS1.p3.pic1.1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.pic1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Attributes:<span id="S5.SS1.p3.pic1.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> [List of Attributes and Values]</span></span></span>
<span id="S5.SS1.p3.pic1.1.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.pic1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Local Context:<span id="S5.SS1.p3.pic1.1.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> [Summary of existing relationships involving the entity]</span></span></span>
<span id="S5.SS1.p3.pic1.1.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.pic1.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Task:<span id="S5.SS1.p3.pic1.1.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Propose a new hyperedge of size [k] that includes the central entity. The group should be semantically coherent and structurally sound based on the context.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) Reviewer Agent Template:</span> This agent assesses the quality and viability of a proposed hyperedge, acting as a quality control mechanism.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.SS1.p5.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="245.46" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 245.46" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,245.46) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 239.56 C 0 242.82 2.64 245.46 5.91 245.46 L 471.47 245.46 C 474.73 245.46 477.38 242.82 477.38 239.56 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 239.56 C 1.97 241.73 3.73 243.5 5.91 243.5 L 471.47 243.5 C 473.65 243.5 475.41 241.73 475.41 239.56 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 11.44 11.44)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:32.85em;--ltx-fo-height:16.29em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.2em;font-size:10pt;" height="228.12" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 225.35)" width="454.55"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS1.p5.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:32.85em;">
<span id="S5.SS1.p5.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p5.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">System prompt:<span id="S5.SS1.p5.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> You are a hypergraph relationship reviewer. Your task is to validate a candidate hyperedge.</span></span></span>
<span id="S5.SS1.p5.pic1.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p5.pic1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">User content:<span id="S5.SS1.p5.pic1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Please review the following candidate hyperedge:</span></span></span>
<span id="S5.SS1.p5.pic1.1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p5.pic1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Candidate Hyperedge:<span id="S5.SS1.p5.pic1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> [List of Entity IDs]</span></span></span>
<span id="S5.SS1.p5.pic1.1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p5.pic1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Entity Details:<span id="S5.SS1.p5.pic1.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> [Summary of attributes for all entities in the candidate hyperedge]</span></span></span>
<span id="S5.SS1.p5.pic1.1.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p5.pic1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Evaluation Criteria:<span id="S5.SS1.p5.pic1.1.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
- Internal Cohesion: Are the members a good fit?
- Network Impact: How does this group affect the overall structure?</span></span></span>
<span id="S5.SS1.p5.pic1.1.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p5.pic1.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Decision:<span id="S5.SS1.p5.pic1.1.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Output "APPROVE" or "REJECT".</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3) Remover Agent Template:</span> This agent prunes the network by identifying and removing stale or low-quality hyperedges, ensuring the long-term health of the hypergraph.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.SS1.p7.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="228.86" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 228.86" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,228.86) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 222.95 C 0 226.22 2.64 228.86 5.91 228.86 L 471.47 228.86 C 474.73 228.86 477.38 226.22 477.38 222.95 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 222.95 C 1.97 225.13 3.73 226.89 5.91 226.89 L 471.47 226.89 C 473.65 226.89 475.41 225.13 475.41 222.95 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 11.44 11.44)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:32.85em;--ltx-fo-height:15.09em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.2em;font-size:10pt;" height="211.52" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 208.75)" width="454.55"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS1.p7.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:32.85em;">
<span id="S5.SS1.p7.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p7.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">System prompt:<span id="S5.SS1.p7.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> You are a hypergraph network curator. Your task is to identify and remove redundant or low-quality hyperedges.</span></span></span>
<span id="S5.SS1.p7.pic1.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p7.pic1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">User content:<span id="S5.SS1.p7.pic1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Analyze the provided list of hyperedges.</span></span></span>
<span id="S5.SS1.p7.pic1.1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p7.pic1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Hyperedges:<span id="S5.SS1.p7.pic1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> [List of existing hyperedges]</span></span></span>
<span id="S5.SS1.p7.pic1.1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p7.pic1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Global Strategy:<span id="S5.SS1.p7.pic1.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> [Current network-wide optimization goal, e.g., "ENHANCE_DIVERSITY"]</span></span></span>
<span id="S5.SS1.p7.pic1.1.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p7.pic1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Task:<span id="S5.SS1.p7.pic1.1.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Identify indices of hyperedges that are redundant, internally incoherent, or conflict with the global strategy. Output indices or "NONE".</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(4) Optimizer Agent Template:</span> This agent performs a global assessment of the evolving hypergraph and provides strategic guidance for other agents during each evolution step. Its decisions are informed by general network statistics and diversity measures rather than domain-specific rules. Based on the current state of the network, the optimizer may, for example, recommend increasing connections, enhancing diversity, reducing clustering, or simply maintaining the existing structure. These strategies are illustrative rather than prescriptive, ensuring the approach remains adaptable across different contexts and data sources.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.SS1.p9.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="212.18" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 212.18" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,212.18) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 206.27 C 0 209.53 2.64 212.18 5.91 212.18 L 471.47 212.18 C 474.73 212.18 477.38 209.53 477.38 206.27 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 206.27 C 1.97 208.45 3.73 210.21 5.91 210.21 L 471.47 210.21 C 473.65 210.21 475.41 208.45 475.41 206.27 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 11.44 11.36)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:32.85em;--ltx-fo-height:13.89em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="194.83" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 192.14)" width="454.55"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS1.p9.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:32.85em;">
<span id="S5.SS1.p9.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p9.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">System prompt:<span id="S5.SS1.p9.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> You are a network strategy analyst. Your task is to assess the entire hypergraph and provide a strategic directive for the next evolution step.</span></span></span>
<span id="S5.SS1.p9.pic1.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p9.pic1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">User content:<span id="S5.SS1.p9.pic1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Analyze the current hypergraph state.</span></span></span>
<span id="S5.SS1.p9.pic1.1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p9.pic1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Network Statistics:<span id="S5.SS1.p9.pic1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> [e.g., density, clustering, component sizes, diversity metrics]</span></span></span>
<span id="S5.SS1.p9.pic1.1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p9.pic1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Task:<span id="S5.SS1.p9.pic1.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Based on the analysis, choose one strategic directive that will most effectively improve the network’s quality and realism.</span></span></span>
<span id="S5.SS1.p9.pic1.1.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p9.pic1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Decision:<span id="S5.SS1.p9.pic1.1.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Output corresponding optimization suggestions.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Generation Algorithms</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">HyperLLM employs two complementary algorithms. The first, <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Iterative Local Generation</span>, is used during the construction phase for rapid, efficient network building. The second, <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-Agent Collaborative Generation</span>, orchestrates the four specialized agents during the evolution phase for fine-grained, high-quality network refinement.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Iterative Local Generation</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l0.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
              
              
             Set of entities <math id="alg1.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, Target number of hyperedges <math id="alg1.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l0a" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l0a.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
              
              
             A foundational hypergraph <math id="alg1.l0a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}=(V,E_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}=(V,E_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
             <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{0}\leftarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{0}\leftarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{c}\leftarrow\textsc{SelectEntity}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">SelectEntity</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{c}\leftarrow\textsc{SelectEntity}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> {Select a central entity}

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leftarrow\textsc{DetermineHyperedgeSize}()" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">DetermineHyperedgeSize</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leftarrow\textsc{DetermineHyperedgeSize}()</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{local}\leftarrow\textsc{GetLocalContext}(v_{c},E_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">GetLocalContext</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{local}\leftarrow\textsc{GetLocalContext}(v_{c},E_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\leftarrow\textsc{BuildGeneratorPrompt}(v_{c},C_{local},k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">BuildGeneratorPrompt</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\leftarrow\textsc{BuildGeneratorPrompt}(v_{c},C_{local},k)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{cand}\leftarrow\textsc{LLMQuery}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">LLMQuery</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{cand}\leftarrow\textsc{LLMQuery}(P)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <span id="alg1.l8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Validate</span>( <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{cand}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{cand}</annotation></semantics></math> ) <span id="alg1.l8.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
               <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{0}\leftarrow E_{0}\cup\{E_{cand}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{0}\leftarrow E_{0}\cup\{E_{cand}\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span>  <math id="alg1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}=(V,E_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}=(V,E_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The multi-agent algorithm forms the core of the evolution phase. In each step, the <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Optimizer</span> agent first assesses the global state of the network and broadcasts a strategic directive. This directive acts as a communication signal that coordinates the actions of the other agents. The <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remover</span> may then prune edges that conflict with the strategy, while the <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generator</span> and <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reviewer</span> work in tandem to create new, high-quality edges that align with the global goal, as detailed in Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 5.2. Generation Algorithms ‣ 5. Hypergraph Generators ‣ Modeling Hypergraph Using Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. This creates a feedback loop where global network health guides local modifications.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> Multi-Agent Collaborative Generation</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l0.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
              
              
             Initial hypergraph <math id="alg2.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{t-1}=(V,E^{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{t-1}=(V,E^{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, Agent set <math id="alg2.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l0a" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l0a.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
              
              
             Evolved hypergraph <math id="alg2.l0a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{t}=(V,E^{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{t}=(V,E^{t})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
             // 1. Global Strategy Formulation

</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
             <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{t}\leftarrow\textsc{Optimizer.Analyze}(H^{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Optimizer.Analyze</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{t}\leftarrow\textsc{Optimizer.Analyze}(H^{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math>
{Optimizer sets global strategy}

</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
             // 2. Network Pruning

</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
             <math id="alg2.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{rem}\leftarrow\textsc{BuildRemoverPrompt}(E^{t-1},\mathcal{S}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">BuildRemoverPrompt</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{rem}\leftarrow\textsc{BuildRemoverPrompt}(E^{t-1},\mathcal{S}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
             <math id="alg2.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{rem}\leftarrow\textsc{Remover.Query}(P_{rem})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Remover.Query</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{rem}\leftarrow\textsc{Remover.Query}(P_{rem})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
             <math id="alg2.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}\leftarrow E^{t-1}\setminus E_{rem}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}\leftarrow E^{t-1}\setminus E_{rem}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
